\end{table}

\subsection{Stage 1: Keyword-Based Compliance Mapping}

For the empirical evaluation reported in this paper, we apply the Stage 1
keyword-based control mapping pipeline exclusively, since it provides a reproducible,
deterministic baseline that requires no LLM API keys and enables full replication of
results. The 22-control keyword dictionary was developed by the first author by mapping each
control's formal NIST definition and associated assessment procedures to domain-specific
keyword phrases (e.g., ``encryption at rest,'' ``disk encryption,'' ``aes,'' ``data at rest''
for SC-02); each control maps to between 3 and 15 keywords. The full keyword dictionary
is provided in the supplementary notebook. A control is marked as \textit{present} if
any associated keyword appears (case-insensitively) in the document text. Prompt lengths
were estimated from representative policy documents in the corpus.

\subsection{Scoring}

To produce comparable, interpretable results across documents of varying length and
scope, we assign each document a single compliance coverage score derived from the
fraction of the 22 ProofLayer controls that are marked present. This score is then
mapped to a risk tier consistent with NIST maturity guidance. The overall compliance
coverage score for each document is computed as:
\begin{equation}
S = \left\lfloor \frac{|\text{matched controls}|}{|\text{total controls}|} \times 100 \right\rfloor
\label{eq:score}
\end{equation}
Documents are classified as \textbf{LOW} risk ($S \geq 80$), \textbf{MEDIUM} risk
($55 \leq S < 80$), or \textbf{HIGH} risk ($S < 55$) based on heuristic thresholds
calibrated to approximate NIST maturity guidance; these specific percentage values
are not prescribed by NIST SP 800-53 directly. The gap frequency for each control is the number of
documents in which that control is absent, providing a measure of systemic weakness
across the policy corpus.

\subsection{Cost Estimation}
\label{sec:cost}

LLM inference costs are estimated using published per-thousand-token pricing for each
model as of mid-2025. For a standard audit (document extraction, keyword mapping, LLM
gap analysis, and narrative report generation), we compare the ProofLayer multi-LLM
strategy against a single-model baseline in which all tasks are routed to
\texttt{gpt-4o}. Estimated input and output token counts are based on ProofLayer's
production prompt templates and typical policy document lengths (extraction: 2,000
input / 400 output tokens; mapping: 1,500 / 300; gap analysis: 3,000 / 700; report:
2,500 / 800; long-document routing via claude-sonnet: 6,700 / 1,000). These are model inputs, not
measured values. Actual costs will vary proportionally with document length. The full keyword dictionary, routing algorithm, cost model, and
analysis code are provided in the supplementary Jupyter notebook.


% ── 5. Experimental Results ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Experimental Results}

We report the results of applying ProofLayer's Stage 1 keyword-based compliance
mapping to the ten-document Maryland policy corpus. Results are organized by
analysis type: aggregate coverage scores, the per-document per-control coverage
heatmap, a systemic gap analysis identifying controls most frequently absent across
the corpus, and a cost analysis comparing the multi-LLM routing strategy against
a single high-capability model baseline.

\subsection{Overall Compliance Coverage}

Figure~\ref{fig:scores} shows the compliance coverage scores for all ten Maryland
policy documents. The mean score across the corpus is 49.1\% (SD = 17.3\%),
substantially below the LOW-risk threshold of 80\%. Eight of ten documents are rated
HIGH or MEDIUM risk. The MD Judicial Information Security Policy achieves the highest
score (86\%), benefiting from broad coverage of access control, encryption, incident
response, and training topics. The MD Procurement Manual scores lowest (22\%), reflecting
its focus on contracting procedures with minimal explicit security control discussion.

\begin{figure}[H]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig2_compliance_scores.pdf}
  \caption{NIST 800-53 / FedRAMP / CMMC coverage scores for ten Maryland government
  policy documents. Green bars indicate LOW risk ($\geq$80\%), orange bars MEDIUM risk
  (55--79\%), and red bars HIGH risk ($<$55\%). Dashed lines mark the LOW and MEDIUM
  risk thresholds. Source: generated by the authors from the analysis pipeline described in Section~\ref{sec:corpus}.}
  \label{fig:scores}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Control Coverage Heatmap}

Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap} presents a control-by-document coverage heatmap, where green
(``P'') cells indicate a control is present and red (``X'') cells indicate a gap.
The heatmap reveals structural patterns: controls related to risk assessment (RA-01,
RA-02), remote access (AC-05), network segmentation (SC-03), and personnel screening
(PS-02) are widely covered, while data encryption (SC-01, SC-02) and formal account
management (AC-01, AC-02) are consistently absent.

\begin{figure}[H]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig3_heatmap.pdf}
  \caption{Per-document per-control coverage heatmap across the 22-control ProofLayer
  dataset. P = control present; X = control missing. Source: generated by the authors from the analysis pipeline described in Section~\ref{sec:corpus}.}
  \label{fig:heatmap}
\end{figure}

