\end{tabular}
\end{table}
\noindent\small\textsuperscript{a}CMMC 2.0 (based on NIST SP 800-171) does not include policy-documentation controls equivalent to NIST AC-1; the AC domain is listed as the closest applicable family.

The most striking finding is the near-universal absence of explicit encryption-at-rest
controls (SC-02): only the MD Judicial Information Security Policy addresses disk
encryption, AES standards, or encrypted storage in any depth. Given that ransomware
attacks on Maryland agencies have involved sensitive records, this gap represents
a potential policy gap for state and local government data assets.

Account management formalism (AC-02) is similarly underrepresented. While many policies
discuss ``access'' at a conceptual level, only one of the ten contains the specific procedural
language (account lifecycle, provisioning and deprovisioning procedures, or role-based
onboarding workflows) needed to satisfy the NIST AC-2 control family. This finding
aligns with the Maryland Cybersecurity Council's 2021 recommendation that the state
adopt centralized account management oversight through the State Chief Information
Security Officer (SCISO)~\cite{md_local_gov_2021}.

Incident reporting obligations (IR-02) requiring notification to CISA within
specific timeframes are absent from eight of ten documents, suggesting that most
Maryland agency policies have not been updated to reflect the mandatory reporting
timelines established under current CISA guidance~\cite{cisa_reporting_2023}.
US-CERT's functions have been incorporated into CISA since 2018, and references
in policy documents to the former entity should be updated accordingly.

\subsection{Cost Analysis}

Figure~\ref{fig:cost} compares per-task costs between the ProofLayer multi-LLM
strategy and a single \texttt{gpt-4o} baseline for a standard document audit.

\begin{figure}[H]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig5_cost_analysis.pdf}
  \caption{(a) Per-task cost comparison between single-model (\texttt{gpt-4o} only) and
  ProofLayer multi-LLM routing. (b) ProofLayer model usage distribution
  across a typical audit session. Source: generated by the authors from the analysis pipeline described in Section~\ref{sec:corpus}.}
  \label{fig:cost}
\end{figure}

{\sloppy Table~\ref{tab:cost_breakdown} quantifies the cost reduction per task.
Routing document extraction and keyword mapping to \texttt{gpt-4o-mini} reduces
costs for those tasks by approximately 97\% compared to \texttt{gpt-4o}, since these
tasks require only token-level pattern recognition rather than complex reasoning.\par} Gap
analysis and report generation are retained on \texttt{gpt-4o}, as these tasks
require multi-hop reasoning over long policy contexts and structured output generation.
Long documents exceeding 15,000 characters are routed to \texttt{claude-sonnet},
which processes the full document context (${\sim}$6,700 input tokens) rather than
the 4,000-token window used in the single-model baseline. The lower per-token price
(\$0.003/1K vs.\ \$0.005/1K) offsets the higher token count, yielding equivalent
per-task cost with improved long-context coverage.
Overall, the multi-LLM strategy achieves an estimated 24\% cost reduction per audit,
driven entirely by routing extraction and mapping to \texttt{gpt-4o-mini}, reducing
the projected per-document cost from approximately \$0.113 to \$0.086.

